.83 80.77 L83.76 79.72 L84.81 78.38 L85.78 77.32 L85.44 76.61 L84.63 75.92 L83.19 75.36 L81.80 74.38 L80.57 73.11 L80.00 71.80 L79.48 70.38 L78.99 69.26 L78.82 68.75 L78.34 68.53 L77.57 67.96 L77.08 67.43 L76.38 67.13 L75.12 66.79 L73.80 66.20 L72.17 64.82 L70.99 63.27 L70.40 62.26 L69.78 61.54 L66.41 60.35 L64.88 58.94 L64.27 59.38 L63.37 58.95 L62.49 58.27 L62.22 57.74 L61.84 57.21 L61.49 56.60 L61.27 56.01 L60.35 54.98 L59.17 53.86 L58.67 53.54 L58.41 53.63 L58.15 54.06 L57.99 54.48 L57.55 54.75 L56.99 55.26 L56.43 55.89 L55.71 56.30 L54.85 56.44 L54.28 56.42 L54.16 56.18 L54.13 55.32 L54.34 53.37 L53.84 52.62 L53.17 51.81 L52.40 50.74 L49.87 48.46 L46.43 45.17 L45.22 44.17 L44.07 44.09 L43.03 44.20 L42.07 44.63 L41.34 46.15 L41.04 46.39 L39.19 46.35 L37.34 46.11 L36.72 45.18 L37.22 44.58 L38.95 43.24 L39.91 42.05 L40.09 41.00 L40.93 40.07 L42.41 39.23 L44.06 37.70 L45.87 35.47 L46.98 33.83 L47.37 32.76 L48.08 31.94 L49.12 31.37 L50.02 30.17 L50.80 28.34 L51.86 26.89 L53.20 25.81 L53.95 24.92 L54.11 24.21 L54.49 23.75 L55.09 23.56 L55.38 23.18 L55.36 22.63 L55.53 22.26 L55.87 22.06 L56.45 22.13 L57.26 22.47 L57.75 22.40 L57.92 21.94 L57.70 21.41 L57.11 20.82 L57.33 20.17 L58.90 19.12 L60.05 18.72 L60.79 18.07 L61.48 17.04 L62.60 16.32 L64.16 15.91 L64.57 15.52 L64.52 15.03 L64.83 14.66 L65.75 14.41 L66.25 13.92 L66.34 13.20 L66.63 12.54 L67.14 11.93 L67.71 11.70 L68.34 11.86 L68.93 11.62 L69.48 11.00 L70.14 10.71 L70.91 10.75 L71.31 10.61 L72.64 10.32 L73.37 9.80 L73.97 8.64 L74.50 8.18 L74.97 8.45 L75.78 8.06 L76.92 7.02Z" fill="#C9473D" stroke="#FFF9E8" stroke-width="0.7" fill-rule="evenodd"></path></svg></div></custom-code><h4 font-size="20pt" align="center">Rio Grande do Sul</h4><p font-size="18pt" align="center">The conflict’s centre.</p></grid-item></grid><p font-size="18pt" role="caption"><b>Modern location guide.</b> Simplified present-day boundaries, not an 1835 border map. Shapes: <a href="https://www.naturalearthdata.com/">Natural Earth</a>.</p></grid-item></grid><notes><p>Trace South America → Brazil → Rio Grande do Sul. Geographic outlines are derived from Natural Earth administrative boundaries. Each panel zooms independently; panel sizes do not compare geographic area. These are modern location guides, not historical borders.</p></notes></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="eGz9WJhQfy"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="IGsZDulPrk" padding="40pt 54pt 28pt"><h2 font-size="34pt">Why were people arguing?</h2><grid marker="icon" marker-size="small" marker-position="inline" spacing="small"><grid-item id="gBEXunHNoi" class="panel" background-color="palette-5" cell-style="solid" padding="14pt 16pt" colspan="30"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/lX0wAQmNtwwd2yriVz5S.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="trade rules arrows and tax paper" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;Two arrows pointing at each other in a circular motion, embodying the concept of trade or exchange.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h4 font-size="22pt">Trade and taxes</h4><p font-size="18pt">Economic rules affected sellers and producers.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="71CweA-d25" class="panel" background-color="palette-6" cell-style="solid" padding="14pt 16pt" colspan="30"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/soptjx1afsFzVYwzPjZN.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="local government decision community" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A solid black color fills the entire space, embodying the concept of local by signifying a singular, unified presence that is not dispersed or divided, much like how a local entity or community is concentrated in one area.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h4 font-size="22pt">Regional power</h4><p font-size="18pt">Some leaders wanted more say nearby.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="ek1Oq97OEQ" class="panel" background-color="palette-7" cell-style="solid" padding="14pt 16pt" colspan="30"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/cvoNv5Qeqq7jTbQAcfjMLT.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="political idea separate republic" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A blank space with a black area, signifying nothingness or emptiness.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h4 font-size="22pt">Political future</h4><p font-size="18pt">Some rebels sought a separate republic.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="L-cH_Kka8K" class="panel" background-color="palette-8" cell-style="solid" padding="14pt 16pt" colspan="30"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/aVohuPeDtwTB6MLCoLYH.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="united country central government" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A black shape of a country map outline in a simple form, set against a clean white background, symbolizing a nation or territory.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h4 font-size="22pt">Imperial government</h4><p font-size="18pt">It wanted Brazil to remain together.</p></grid-item></grid><p font-size="18pt" role="caption"><b>No single cause:</b> people made different choices for different reasons. <b>Source:</b> <a href="https://cultura.rs.gov.br/upload/arquivos/carga20210405/27100547-anais-ahrs-v-16-1.pdf">State Historical Archive of Rio Grande do Sul</a></p></grid-item></grid><notes><p>Read the causes as connected disagreements. Avoid sorting everyone into heroes and villains; one person could care about several issues at once.</p></notes></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="aDMqCrl1XQ"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="WfSBo8XDI7" padding="42pt 58pt 32pt"><h2 font-size="34pt">1835 → 1845: one long conflict</h2><p font-size="20pt">The middle of a timeline can matter as much as its endpoints.</p><grid marker="number" marker-style="outline" marker-shape="circle" marker-position="above" spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="Ieree7VnEM" class="panel" background-color="palette-5" padding="16pt" colspan="20"><h3 font-size="26pt">1835</h3><p font-size="18pt">The conflict begins in Rio Grande do Sul.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="LSfYW9GIMT" class="panel" background-color="palette-6" padding="16pt" colspan="20"><h3 font-size="26pt">Across 10 years</h3><p font-size="18pt">Families and workers live with uncertainty.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="lAI7b_GsIM" class="panel" background-color="palette-7" padding="16pt" colspan="20"><h3 font-size="26pt">1845</h3><p font-size="18pt">The conflict ends.</p></grid-item></grid><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="xceDt1ZNPg" class="panel" background-color="surface" padding="12pt 18pt"><p font-size="19pt"><b>Time check:</b> Ten years is longer than most children spend in primary school.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid><notes><p>Point first to the endpoints, then to the centre. The centre represents years of ordinary life continuing under uncertainty; it is not a separate dated event.</p></notes></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="4p4qW4vxL9"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="sSYgGEtB-c" padding="30pt 50pt 28pt"><p font-size="18pt" role="caption"><b>ILLUSTRATIVE VIEWPOINTS · NOT REAL BIOGRAPHIES</b></p><h2 font-size="32pt">The same years felt different to different people</h2><grid spacing="small" image-shape="rounded"><grid-item id="WdK_iV5QVJ" class="panel disc" background-color="palette-5" padding="12pt" colspan="15"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/bdvnf_vWeu6leX_noyB9M.png" saved-media-id="0exff7f0b7mounh" source="image.ai-generated" height="105pt" image-style="gouache-cutout" alt="One full-body faceless adult woman market seller standing with an empty hand basket, simple 1830s southern Brazil-inspired plain clothing, dignified child-friendly matte gouache cutout, warm marigold accents, transparent background, no readable text, no flags, no food, no weapons" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1024,&quot;height&quot;:1024,&quot;has_transparency&quot;:true}"><h4 font-size="20pt">Rosa · free seller</h4><p font-size="18pt">She watches for changes to trade.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="jMXo-RpgSz" class="panel disc" background-color="palette-6" padding="12pt" colspan="15"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/FA397Da7v-7-1JQMW-YcP.png" saved-media-id="uusbh9x7pkb987w" source="image.ai-generated" height="105pt" image-style="gouache-cutout" alt="One full-body faceless adult rural worker standing upright with a rolled cloth bundle, simple 1830s southern Brazil-inspired plain work clothing, dignified child-friendly matte gouache cutout, leaf green accents, transparent background, no readable text, no flags, no weapons" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1024,&quot;height&quot;:1024,&quot;has_transparency&quot;:true}"><h4 font-size="20pt">Tomás · rural worker</h4><p font-size="18pt">He thinks about work, food, and travel.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="21PMUmMU9X" class="panel disc" background-color="palette-7" padding="12pt" colspan="15"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/EPbgUbf3R-MPb2qdb28vG.png" saved-media-id="9gsc2ut84muy5y7" source="image.ai-generated" height="105pt" image-style="gouache-cutout" alt="One full-body faceless enslaved adult woman standing upright with calm dignity and holding a folded plain cloth, historically restrained simple clothing, child-friendly matte gouache cutout, brick red accents, transparent background, no chains, no caricature, no readable text, no flags, no weapons" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1024,&quot;height&quot;:1024,&quot;has_transparency&quot;:true}"><h4 font-size="20pt">Luzia · enslaved worker</h4><p font-size="18pt">She has fewer rights; the revolt does not end slavery.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="kgprbSCFAG" class="panel disc" background-color="palette-8" padding="12pt" colspan="15"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/7NHTewicQl8gMwwSjudqq.png" saved-media-id="vwenvj2p036eh1h" source="image.ai-generated" height="105pt" image-style="gouache-cutout" alt="One full-body faceless child in simple plain historical clothing listening with one hand near an ear, curious but calm pose, child-friendly matte gouache cutout, sky blue accents, transparent background, no readable text, no flags, no weapons" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1024,&quot;height&quot;:1024,&quot;has_transparency&quot;:true}"><h4 font-size="20pt">Davi · child</h4><p font-size="18pt">He hears news shaped by adults around him.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid><notes><p>These figures support comparison; they are not invented witnesses. Treat Luzia with dignity and emphasize that rights and freedom were deeply unequal.</p></notes></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="HBwInwJ782"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="gjj4_E6mFW" padding="36pt 54pt 28pt"><p font-size="18pt" role="caption"><b>ILLUSTRATIVE OBJECTS · NOT ARCHIVAL ARTIFACTS</b></p><h2 font-size="32pt">Everyday objects carry big questions</h2><grid spacing="small" image-shape="rounded"><grid-item id="HjpVvf0vya" class="panel clue" background-color="palette-5" padding="12pt" colspan="15"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/QJ_oFFCICxSCxjiI95XvK.png" saved-media-id="34i1v9n37ayqcue" source="image.ai-generated" height="100pt" image-style="gouache-cutout" alt="One blank historical tax paper with a small plain wax seal, slightly creased, matte gouache cutout with gentle shadow, warm marigold detail, transparent background, no readable text, no currency, no people" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1024,&quot;height&quot;:1024,&quot;has_transparency&quot;:true}"><h4 font-size="22pt">Tax paper</h4><p font-size="18pt">Who pays—and who decides the rules?</p></grid-item><grid-item id="VTwr9Iz-Ps" class="panel clue" background-color="palette-6" padding="12pt" colspan="15"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/_DZos2bF3B8VcNdsiIn8N.png" saved-media-id="cdtzhrsonj0ox3b" source="image.ai-generated" height="100pt" image-style="gouache-cutout" alt="One unlabeled folded paper map opened into three panels, matte gouache cutout with leaf green and muted blue patches, transparent background, no readable text, no flags, no people" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1024,&quot;height&quot;:1024,&quot;has_transparency&quot;:true}"><h4 font-size="22pt">Map</h4><p font-size="18pt">Who should govern the region?</p></grid-item><grid-item id="zO6mh9zZ7r" class="panel clue" background-color="palette-7" padding="12pt" colspan="15"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/TcmIhgISYlTqIxktE_i65.png" saved-media-id="b5v3xsuammaeedz" source="image.ai-generated" height="100pt" image-style="gouache-cutout" alt="One sealed historical letter envelope tied with plain string, matte gouache cutout with brick red seal and gentle shadow, transparent background, no readable text, no people" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1024,&quot;height&quot;:1024,&quot;has_transparency&quot;:true}"><h4 font-size="22pt">Letter</h4><p font-size="18pt">How do people learn what is happening?</p></grid-item><grid-item id="kzFzRNngJh" class="panel clue" background-color="palette-8" padding="12pt" colspan="15"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/GBHHrmWIojzGg5pn3lZsz.png" saved-media-id="27x6hs9yv7w9cyf" source="image.ai-generated" height="100pt" image-style="gouache-cutout" alt="One empty woven household basket with two simple handles, matte gouache cutout with sky blue cloth tucked at one edge, transparent background, no food, no readable text, no people" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1024,&quot;height&quot;:1024,&quot;has_transparency&quot;:true}"><h4 font-size="22pt">Food basket</h4><p font-size="18pt">How do families keep daily life going?</p></grid-item></grid><p font-size="19pt"><b>Detective rule:</b> an object is a clue, not a complete answer.</p></grid-item></grid><notes><p>Ask what each object might reveal and what it cannot reveal alone. Historians compare objects with other evidence and viewpoints.</p></notes></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="NZYHjS174U"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="5E7gmk71qV" padding="40pt 56pt 28pt"><h2 font-size="34pt">What changed—and what did not?</h2><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="9A1WpVnf9L" class="panel" background-color="palette-6" cell-style="solid" padding="20pt" colspan="22"><labels class="tag"><label variant="solid" color="var(--palette-2)">Changed</label></labels><h3 font-size="28pt">The conflict ended</h3><p font-size="19pt">The ten-year conflict ended in 1845.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="0PgBblEX9B" class="panel" background-color="palette-7" cell-style="solid" padding="20pt" colspan="38"><labels class="tag"><label variant="solid" color="var(--palette-3)">Did not change</label></labels><h4 font-size="22pt">Brazil stayed together</h4><p font-size="18pt">Rio Grande do Sul did not remain separate.</p><h4 font-size="22pt">Slavery continued</h4><p font-size="18pt">Abolition was not an outcome of the revolt.</p></grid-item></grid><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="_J-PWpkFFP" class="panel" background-color="palette-8" cell-style="solid" padding="12pt 18pt"><p font-size="19pt"><b>Big idea:</b> ending a conflict does not automatically make life fair for everyone.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid><notes><p>Separate the end of the conflict from the idea that every problem was solved. Return to Luzia’s viewpoint: slavery continued, so the ending did not bring equal freedom.</p></notes></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="2sSD3lwUtF"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="kWj36iqkVd" padding="32pt 56pt 24pt"><h2 font-size="34pt">History detective quiz</h2><p font-size="18pt">Think first. Then check the answer key.</p><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="cs1xMs0gHX" colspan="36"><grid divider="sm neutral-border" marker="number" marker-style="outline" marker-shape="circle" marker-position="inline" direction="vertical" vertical-sizing="hug" style="--marker-inline-size: 30pt; --marker-gap: 12pt;"><grid-item id="L7yxEONY7h" padding="8pt 0"><h4 font-size="20pt">Which Brazilian state is at the centre of this story?</h4></grid-item><grid-item id="SXPxcag8Sm" padding="8pt 0"><h4 font-size="20pt">Name two disagreements behind the conflict.</h4></grid-item><grid-item id="reUzW2rZsE" padding="8pt 0"><h4 font-size="20pt">Why could the same years feel different to different people?</h4></grid-item></grid></grid-item><grid-item id="syULIy9kCz" class="panel" background-color="surface" padding="16pt" colspan="24"><h3 font-size="24pt">Answer key</h3><grid marker="number" marker-position="inline" marker-numbering-suffix="." direction="vertical" spacing="small" vertical-sizing="hug" style="--marker-inline-size: 18pt; --marker-gap: 7pt; --marker-font-size: 17pt;"><grid-item id="UCOXGFayVx" padding="4pt 0"><p font-size="17pt">Rio Grande do Sul.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="dbFhVLD8Qp" padding="4pt 0"><p font-size="17pt">Any two: trade and taxes, regional power, or a separate republic.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="mM_IPR0Zhz" padding="4pt 0"><p font-size="17pt">Rights and choices were unequal.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="jXps9k6noq" class="panel" marker="none" background-color="palette-8" padding="9pt 12pt"><p font-size="17pt"><b>Last clue:</b> Whose voice is still missing—and what evidence might help us find it?</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid><notes><p>Let children answer aloud before reading the key. End with the missing-voices question so the lesson closes on evidence, perspective, and curiosity rather than simple recall.</p></notes></section>